user_prompt:
Please extract the key user guidance and Q&A on records management procedures from the City of Portland Records Center document and format it into JSON following the provided schema.

=== Report ===
Effective records management is essential for maintaining organizational efficiency and compliance. One central aspect for users in a records center environment is the precise assignment of Schedule Numbers to records before their transfer. The guidance provided to users underscores the importance of correctly identifying the applicable retention schedule and urges individuals to consult either a Records Analyst or the City Archivist if uncertainty arises.

Clear instruction is given on where bureaus can locate their relevant schedules: these are organized alphabetically online, accessible without an intranet login, ensuring broad and convenient access. For users unable to find a suitable, bureau-specific schedule for their record types, reference is made to the City’s General Schedules, which serve as a fallback but are not intended as the primary resource. Direct contact information for further support is provided, indicating an emphasis on user assistance and cross-department collaboration.

Another recurring concern among records center users is the management of mixed or multifaceted collections. The instructions strongly advise against assigning a single representative schedule to a box containing heterogeneous record types. Instead, users are expected to sort and document each record set distinctly, reflecting a rigorous approach to records tracking within the Physical Archives and Records Center (PARC). Support for sorting and scheduling is offered, highlighting a commitment to proper record lifecycle management.

In decisions about storage or disposition—such as freeing up filing cabinet space—the same diligence is mandated. Each record should be matched to its retention schedule before any disposal action is taken, ensuring that mandated periods and confidentiality requirements are observed. This approach acknowledges the presence of records with permanent value and those requiring confidential handling, reinforcing the reliance on established schedules for guidance.

These operational policies and procedures are summarized in the following table, which presents typical user questions alongside authoritative responses, demonstrating the structured support available within the records center environment: ## Sheet: Sheet1

| Unnamed: 1                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Unnamed: 5   |
|:------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-------------|
| RECORDS CENTER USER:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | nan          |
| It is very important that you assign the correct Schedule Number to your records prior to transferring them from your office.  If you are unsure which schedule should be assigned to your records, please contact the Records Analyst, [Person_1], at x 5-4107 or the City Archivist, [Person_2], at x 5-4110.                                                                                                   | nan          |
| Please take a moment to read through the questions and answers below.  If you have a question that is not covered by those listed, please email it to:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            | nan          |
| [USER]@portlandoregon.gov                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | nan          |
| nan                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |              |
| What is [Person_3]?                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | nan          |
| A Retention Schedule describes the records created by an agency, organization, or administrative unit and establishes a timetable for their life cycle, as well as providing authorization for their disposition.                                                                                                                                                                                                 | nan          |
| Where do I find my bureau's Schedules?                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            | nan          |
| They are found, alphabetically, online (on the Internet and [Person_4]).  The link provided below will take you to the Schedules on the Internet site; therefore, you will not be prompted to Login to the City's Intranet.                                                                                                                                                                                       | nan          |
| http://www.portlandonline.com/auditor/index.cfm?c=51813                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | nan          |
| I searched through all of the record types listed within my bureau/division and did not find [Person_3] that accurately describes my records.  What should I do?                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | nan          |
| You should then refer to the City's GENERAL Schedules which can be used by all City employees.  These schedules should only be used if the appropriate bureau-specific schedule is not found.  If you are still not finding the schedule that describes your records, contact [Person_1] or [Person_2] (see gray box above).                                                                                      | nan          |
| The records I need to send to archives appear to be a mixture of many different record types.  May I pack them all in one box and assign [Person_5] that represents the majority of the records?                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | nan          |
| Definitely not.  In order to properly manage the records, and track them while they reside here at PARC, you'll need to be sure that the data assigned to each box accurately represents the records inside.  In such a case, you will need to sort the records before you file them in the boxes.  If you need assistance sorting and scheduling your records, please don't hesitate to contact us.              | nan          |
| I need to make room in my filing cabinet.  Should I assign [Person_3] to the records before I dispose of them?                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    | nan          |
| Yes;  there is no other way to know when or how to dispose of your records except by locating the records' State-approved [Person_6]. There are many record types that have a "permanent" retention and will never be destroyed; others may require a confidential disposition.  The only way to know how to handle your records is by referring to the Retention Schedule that has been created for your record. | nan          |
. 

Collectively, this approach creates a robust framework that empowers users to manage records with confidence, ensures compliance with state-mandated requirements, and preserves critical institutional memory.

