=== Report ===
An analysis of genetic variation across Melanesian island populations reveals distinct patterns of population structure when examining microsatellite data. The analysis encompasses 687 microsatellite markers, assessing how genetic diversity is distributed both within and among groups inhabiting the region’s various islands.

Across the broad scope of Island Melanesia, which aggregates data from 35 populations, the variance among populations accounts for approximately 4.6% of the total genetic variation, with the vast majority—95.4%—found within individual populations. This indicates that genetic differences between populations are relatively modest compared to the diversity held within each population.

A closer look at specific island groups highlights subtle but noteworthy differences. The New Britain populations (20 in total) show a slightly higher proportion of genetic variance among populations at 5.0%, suggesting somewhat greater differentiation between groups inhabiting this island compared to the overall regional average. Conversely, Bougainville’s four populations demonstrate a lower among-populations variance of 3.7%, with over 96% of genetic variation residing within populations, indicating relatively homogenous genetics on this island.

In contrast, New Ireland’s eight populations display an even more pronounced within-population genetic diversity, with only 2.0% of total variation occurring among populations. This trend reaches its extreme in New Guinea, where data from three populations show just 0.7% of variance among populations, and a striking 99.3% contained within populations. Such a distribution suggests a highly panmictic genetic structure in New Guinea, possibly reflecting extensive gene flow or recent common ancestry.

The reported confidence intervals for each estimate are narrow, underscoring the robustness of these variance components. These findings collectively underscore the predominance of within-population genetic variability in Melanesian island groups, while emphasizing varying degrees of population structure depending on the island examined.

