Please extract the employee work hour records from early 2018, including UserID, Name, Period, EarnCode, and Hours, and generate a JSON formatted according to the given structure. Also, include the calculated overtime hours for each employee and period as outlined in the example table.

=== Report ===
Over the course of two consecutive biweekly pay periods in early 2018, there is a clear indication of significant variation in recorded work hours among several employees, with notable differences both between individuals and within their own time allocations across these intervals. The records reflect hours categorized under "Regular" and "Lunch" earn codes, allowing for an analysis of direct work time alongside break periods.

Examining individual contributions, one observes that [Person_1] maintained a remarkably consistent number of regular hours, clocking just over 31.8 hours in both periods. In contrast, [Person_2] demonstrated a substantial increase in regular hours worked from approximately 32.6 to 62.5 hours and also increased their lunch break time from about 5.1 to 8.9 hours, suggesting an intensification of work commitments during the latter period. Similarly, [Person_4] logged a substantial rise in regular hours from roughly 63.1 to 75.2 along with a slight decrease in lunch hours from 10.3 to 8.7 hours, indicating a shift toward longer work durations with reduced break time.

Another standout is Goege Fly, whose regular work hours climbed from nearly 69 to over 79 hours between the two periods, highlighting a growing workload. By contrast, [Person_6] appears only in the second period with a modest 7.6 regular hours, suggesting part-time or limited engagement during this timeframe.

The data also imply differing work intensity patterns among other contributors such as [Person_3], who logged 39.4 regular hours in the first period but is absent in the subsequent interval, and [Person_5], who escalated from 67.7 to 76.5 hours in regular time, reflecting increased involvement. Lunch periods varied accordingly, emphasizing diverse break-taking behaviors among employees.

