=== Report ===
The calculation of end-of-service benefits (EOSB) involves nuanced differentiation based on the nature of an employee’s departure and their tenure. Understanding these variations is crucial to ensure compliance with established labor guidelines and to maintain equitable treatment of departing employees.

For employees whose service ends due to termination, the entitlement depends primarily on the length of continuous service. Those serving up to one year are generally not eligible for EOSB. For periods extending from one to five years, the benefit is calculated at a rate of 21 days' salary for each completed year of service. If the employee’s tenure reaches or exceeds five years, the entitlement increases to 31 days' salary per completed year. This graduated scale acknowledges longer service commitments with proportionally greater benefits.

Resignation cases are treated differently, reflecting a more moderate benefit model. Workers who resign within one year are typically ineligible for any EOSB. Those with between one and three years of service receive one third of the entitlement that would be due under the termination slabs. For employees with service spanning three to five years, the entitlement rises to two thirds of the equivalent termination amount. Only after five years of service do resigning employees qualify for the full EOSB benefit, aligning their credit with the termination scale.

The formula for these computations integrates monthly basic salary and days’ rates corresponding to the length of service, applied distinctly depending on the termination or resignation status. For example, for an employee terminated after 1–5 years of service with a monthly salary of 5,000 units, the compensation is based on 21 days per year of service; beyond five years, 31 days per year apply.

