This report examines a portfolio of network connectivity options, focusing on throughput, media type, and associated costs across a range of access speeds and technologies. The offerings span traditional copper Ethernet up to high-throughput fiber solutions, with distinctions between non-recurring installation charges and periodic service fees. Attention to the cost structure across these classes is essential for stakeholders evaluating network upgrades or expansions.

Among the copper Ethernet options, access begins at a modest 10 MG throughput, carrying an initial fee of $1,039 and monthly charges just under $400, accumulating to an annual cost of $4,793. Stepping up to higher copper throughput—specifically, 51 MG—doubles the ongoing cost without increasing the initial fee, with annual expenses at $8,330. There is also a 100 MG copper Ethernet variant, which observes a proportional increase, demonstrating a continued scaling of both monthly and one-time costs for moderate bandwidth increments.

Transitioning to fiber-based solutions, the pattern of cost escalation with throughput becomes more pronounced. Entry-level fiber at 155 MG commands a non-recurring fee of $3,022 and rises sharply to over $15,000 annually. Intermediate fiber options systematically scale through increments (207 MG, 259 MG, 311 MG, and so forth) with both monthly and annual charges climbing accordingly, culminating in the top listed throughput of 933 MG at an annual price exceeding $66,000. Fiber solutions are presented as available on either multimode or singlemode interfaces, appealing to different infrastructure requirements.

The service catalogue also includes traditional TDM connections such as DS-1 (1.544Mb/s) and DS-3 (45Mb/s) on copper T1, with initial and recurring costs reflecting their legacy status and lower bandwidth. For organizations with specialized needs, SONET options (OC-3, OC-12, and OC-48) leverage singlemode fiber and offer standardized throughputs—however, some entries lack complete pricing data.

