Examining individual statements, high levels of agreement in 2008/9—particularly in areas such as “sufficient opportunities to contact staff” (over 92%) and the clarity/accuracy of module information (both above 85%)—point to initial strengths in communication and support structures. However, these dimensions experienced some of the steepest declines in 2009/0, with agreement on staff accessibility dropping by nearly 20 percentage points. Similarly, satisfaction with teaching quality and staff ability to explain concepts initially rated highly, before declining sharply, highlighting potential disruptions or structural changes between the first two years.

Feedback mechanisms and student use of supported open learning consistently drew lower ratings across all years. Perceptions of the usefulness and timeliness of feedback remained below 77% in any year, dipping notably in 2009/0. The trend is mirrored in students’ acknowledgement of having made full use of resources, which never exceeded 71%, and hovered in the mid- to high-60s by the end of the period.

Focusing on the 2010/1 figures, the incremental improvements compared to 2009/0 suggest responsive adjustments may have been implemented. Nevertheless, many items did not return to their initial 2008/9 levels. Satisfaction with module organization, clarity of marking criteria, and communication of changes all improved in the last year, but typically remained five to fifteen points below their starting benchmarks.

