An analysis of priorities in transportation infrastructure investment reveals significant variation across the political spectrum, reflecting divergent policy preferences among parties. When asked where increased funds should be allocated within the transportation sector, respondents aligned with different parties demonstrated distinct emphases on infrastructure types.

Notably, respondents associated with the Green Party exhibited the strongest preference for investments beyond traditional road infrastructure, allocating only about 5% of their priority to road infrastructure enhancement. This contrasts sharply with supporters of the Swiss People’s Party (SVP), who indicated a majority preference—53%—for focusing on road infrastructure improvements. Other parties fall within a moderate range; for instance, the FDP/Liberale and CVP supporters displayed a more balanced approach, with road infrastructure shares of approximately 38% and 28% respectively, while EDU and EVP supporters lean toward lower road infrastructure shares, near or just above 10%.

Support for broader transportation investment—encompassing public transit, cycling, or environmentally friendly modalities—is inferred by the residual share remaining after road infrastructure and undecided responses are accounted for. For example, the Greenliberale and PdAS parties show pronounced emphasis on such alternatives, given their low allocation to road infrastructure and relatively high overall investment interest.

The degree of uncertainty or neutrality among voters is also insightful. The proportion expressing a neutral stance or uncertainty about investment focus varies from a low of around 5% among PdAS affiliates up to nearly 23% within the EDU group. This suggests that some electorates are less decisive or possibly less informed regarding transportation policy priorities.

Overall, the aggregate data suggests that across all responses, about 26% of transportation investment emphasis is placed on road infrastructure, while a majority of about 64% indicate a preference for other forms of infrastructure support or enhancement. This collective view supports a general trend toward diversified investment in transportation beyond expanding road capacity alone.

